WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.828
Zero-one knapsack asks which subset of items maximizes value without exceeding a capacity limit.

00:00:06.828 --> 00:00:12.518
A naive recursion branches into two choices per item and explodes exponentially.

00:00:12.518 --> 00:00:20.200
Dynamic programming records the best result for each reachable weight so overlapping states are solved once.

00:00:20.458 --> 00:00:26.344
In a one-dimensional DP array, iterate capacity from maximum down to the item's weight.

00:00:26.344 --> 00:00:35.070
Sweeping backward ensures each transition reads values from the previous item's layer rather than reusing the current item twice.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.858
Memory drops to a single flat list of length W plus one.

00:00:39.125 --> 00:00:47.148
When each item or coin can be chosen an unlimited number of times, reverse the inner loop to run forward from weight to W.

00:00:47.148 --> 00:00:53.001
Forward iteration intentionally builds upon states that already include the current item.

00:00:53.001 --> 00:00:57.605
Always check whether item reuse is allowed before ordering your loops.

00:00:57.875 --> 00:01:06.027
For boolean subset sum queries in Python, represent all reachable sums as set bits inside a single big integer.

00:01:06.027 --> 00:01:14.400
Processing a weight w becomes a single bitwise shift and OR operation: bits equals bits OR bits shifted left by w.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.366
Python shifts arbitrary-precision integers at C speed.
